== Reward Shaping and Proxy Reward Misalignment

Ng et al. @ng1999reward proved that only potential-based reward shaping preserves optimal policies. Departures from this constraint produce degenerate behaviors, as shown by Randlov and Alstrom @randlov1998bicycle, where a naive shaping reward caused a bicycle-balancing agent to ride in circles collecting intermediate reward rather than reaching the goal. Wiewiora et al. @wiewiora2003pbrs established equivalence between potential-based shaping and Q-value initialization, and Devlin and Kudenko @devlin2012dynamic extended the framework to dynamic, time-dependent potentials while preserving optimality guarantees. These results delineate a narrow corridor within which auxiliary rewards remain safe.

The broader phenomenon of proxy objectives diverging from true objectives has been formalized under several frameworks. Amodei et al. @amodei2016concrete identified reward hacking as a core challenge in AI safety. Manheim and Garrabrant @manheim2018goodhart categorized this failure through Goodhart's Law, distinguishing regressional, extremal, causal, and adversarial variants. Skalse et al. @skalse2022reward proved that reward hacking is inevitable whenever the proxy and true reward are not ordinally equivalent. Gao et al. @gao2023scaling demonstrated this empirically through scaling laws for reward model overoptimization, revealing an inverted-U relationship in which true reward initially rises but then declines as proxy optimization increases. Pan et al. @pan2022reward further showed that reward misspecification produces predictable failure patterns.

These results directly inform our analysis. The IoU-based reward used to train SAM prompt policies is a proxy for segmentation quality that correlates with true mask quality under moderate optimization but eventually induces policy collapse. The agent learns placements that inflate the IoU score without improving, and sometimes degrading, the resulting segmentation, consistent with the inverted-U scaling law of Gao et al. @gao2023scaling and the extremal Goodhart failure described by Manheim and Garrabrant @manheim2018goodhart.
